1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,074
Python has a

2
00:00:01,074 --> 00:00:02,339
built-in array type.

3
00:00:02,339 --> 00:00:04,303
Most people never use it.

4
00:00:04,303 --> 00:00:05,037
Import it from

5
00:00:05,037 --> 00:00:06,504
the array module.

6
00:00:06,554 --> 00:00:07,616
The first argument,

7
00:00:07,616 --> 00:00:09,095
'i', is the type code.

8
00:00:09,095 --> 00:00:10,185
It means every item

9
00:00:10,185 --> 00:00:11,925
is a signed integer.

10
00:00:11,975 --> 00:00:13,438
a.itemsize says each

11
00:00:13,438 --> 00:00:15,662
item takes four bytes.

12
00:00:15,712 --> 00:00:16,776
It works like a list,

13
00:00:16,776 --> 00:00:18,287
so you can append.

14
00:00:18,437 --> 00:00:20,377
But try appending 2.5.

15
00:00:20,377 --> 00:00:21,742
The array refuses.

16
00:00:21,742 --> 00:00:23,765
It only holds integers.

17
00:00:24,115 --> 00:00:24,939
So pick the type

18
00:00:24,939 --> 00:00:26,114
code for your data.

19
00:00:26,114 --> 00:00:27,636
'b' for tiny integers,

20
00:00:27,636 --> 00:00:28,823
'i' for integers,

21
00:00:28,823 --> 00:00:30,610
'd' for decimals.

22
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:31,986
Recap.

23
00:00:31,986 --> 00:00:33,031
An array stores one

24
00:00:33,031 --> 00:00:34,368
type, packed tightly.

25
00:00:34,368 --> 00:00:35,049
The type code

26
00:00:35,049 --> 00:00:36,039
sets that type.

27
00:00:36,039 --> 00:00:36,978
And the wrong type

28
00:00:36,978 --> 00:00:38,539
raises a TypeError.

29
00:00:38,889 --> 00:00:40,894
Next up: NumPy arrays.

30
00:00:40,894 --> 00:00:42,831
Follow for the whole series.
